; SHCL - Simple Hierarchical Config Language
; Formal grammar (ABNF, RFC 5234 + RFC 7405 case-sensitive strings).
;
; ABNF cannot express indentation sensitivity or fenced-block termination on
; its own; those are context-sensitive and are specified in prose in spec.md
; (see "Structure & hierarchy" and "Raw blocks"). This grammar covers the
; line-level and value-level syntax. Where a rule depends on runtime context
; (current indent stack, active fence), that dependency is noted inline.
;
; The escape mark is U+25C9, written %x25C9 below. The escape names, the bare
; text class and the formatter's bare class are generated from one list by
; cicd/utility/gen-escapes.py, in the block near the end.

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; File
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

file            = *line [ line-body ]           ; the last line may lack a newline
line            = line-body newline
line-body       = blank-line / comment-line / field-line / list-item-line / fence-line / raw-content-line
                ; raw-content-line only occurs while a fence is open (context).

blank-line      = *blank [ comment ]
comment-line    = *blank comment
comment         = "#" *non-newline           ; to end of line; any "#" outside quotes,
                                              ; backticks and a raw body opens one,
                                              ; wherever it sits (x#y is x and a
                                              ; comment). An escape mark in a comment
                                              ; is plain text.

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Field lines
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; A field-line's leading whitespace (indent) is significant and governs
; hierarchy per the indentation rules in spec.md. The dotted/bracketed path
; below is the inline-hierarchy sugar; it is exactly equivalent to nesting.

field-line      = indent path *blank ":" *blank [ value ] *blank [ comment ]
                / indent path *blank ":" *blank fence-open *blank [ comment ]   ; raw block, same-line spelling
                ; Semantic (not expressible in ABNF): a selector on the LAST path
                ; segment already supplies that instance's value, so a trailing
                ; value (field(disc): value) is grammar-legal but not a valid
                ; binding - the instance is created from the discriminator and the
                ; value is reported (Error) and dropped. See spec.md "Selectors".
                ; Semantic likewise: the wildcard selector (*) is query-only. On
                ; a binding line it is grammar-legal but reported (Error,
                ; "wildcard selector is query-only") and the whole line skipped.
                ; An index-sel on a binding line selects an existing instance
                ; by position and creates nothing: with no instance there the
                ; line is reported (Error E003) and dropped, since a file names
                ; an instance by its value, not by a position the next edit
                ; could move.
                ; Semantic too: a field with lines under it takes a scalar or
                ; nothing. An array there is E028.

indent          = *( SP / HTAB )              ; one kind per subtree (see spec)

path            = segment *( *blank "." *blank segment )
segment         = field-name *blank [ selector ]
                ; a "(" right after the name (blank allowed) is a selector; a "["
                ; after the ":" starts the value, an array. The same path syntax
                ; is the query syntax, so a file line and a Get() read
                ; identically. A "[" right after the name is the old selector
                ; spelling: E029, kept as written, and refused in a query.
selector        = "(" *blank sel-body *blank ")"
sel-body        = index-sel / value-sel
index-sel       = [ "+" ] 1*DIGIT              ; bare integer = index
                ; a leading "+" is accepted (list(+1) = list(1)); "-" is not
                ; an index sign - (-1) is a value selector. The old "[#N]"
                ; index is gone: a bare body that starts with "#" opens a
                ; comment on a file line and finds nothing in a query.
value-sel       = quoted / bare-sel           ; non-numeric bare, or quoted (forces value)
bare-sel        = 1*( sel-text / escape )
                ; a bare selector's scan runs to the first ")", so a "." passes
                ; through (base(St.Paul).p resolves). It is stricter than a
                ; value: whitespace, a quote, a ":", a ",", a bracket or a
                ; paren in it is E025, a quote that opens it and never closes
                ; is E017, and a "#" opens a comment, which leaves the
                ; selector unterminated (E014). To embed any of those, quote
                ; the discriminator: base("a)b"), base("New York"),
                ; host("a:b"). A selector matches one plain value, never an
                ; array or a raw block.

field-name      = quoted / bare-name
bare-name       = ALPHA *name-char
name-char       = ALPHA / DIGIT / "-" / "_"
                ; a bare name starts with an ASCII letter. One that breaks only
                ; that spelling rule (404, -x, _id, a non-ASCII letter) is E014;
                ; it still reads, so the lines under it load under that name.
                ; A quoted name can be any text, and resolves escapes.

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Values
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Type is NOT encoded in syntax; every value is stored as raw text and typed
; on read by the consumer (accessor-driven). An array is the only value-level
; structural distinction. Numbers/bools/dates are recognized only at read time
; by the coercion rules in spec.md. A backslash is plain text everywhere.

value           = array / scalar
array           = "[" *blank [ element *( *blank "," *blank element ) *blank ] "]"
                ; one line, no nesting, no empty element. "[]" (blanks allowed
                ; inside) is the empty array. Text after the "]", a bare "[" or
                ; "]" inside, an empty element ([a,, b], [a, ]) or no closing
                ; "]" is E019.
element         = quoted / backticked / bare-elem
bare-elem       = *":" bare-unit *( *":" bare-unit )
                ; every "," splits inside the brackets, with or without a blank
                ; after it, so [a,b] is 2 elements. A bare element holds no
                ; whitespace and does not end in ":" (E025).
scalar          = quoted / backticked / bareword

quoted          = dquoted / squoted
dquoted         = DQUOTE *( escape / dqchar ) DQUOTE
squoted         = SQUOTE *( escape / sqchar ) SQUOTE
dqchar          = %x00-09 / %x0B-21 / %x23-25C8 / %x25CA-10FFFF
                ; anything but LF, DQUOTE and the escape mark
sqchar          = %x00-09 / %x0B-26 / %x28-25C8 / %x25CA-10FFFF
                ; anything but LF, "'" and the escape mark
                ; The two quotes differ only in which one each can hold as
                ; text. A quoted piece is quoted only when its first char is
                ; the quote and the matching quote that closes it is the last
                ; thing before the piece ends (a ",", a "]", a comment or the
                ; line end). A piece that opens one and never closes it that
                ; way is E017: the line is kept as written and binds nothing.
backticked      = "`" *btchar "`"
btchar          = %x00-09 / %x0B-5F / %x61-10FFFF
                ; anything but LF and a backtick. Raw: no escapes, and an
                ; escape mark, "#", ",", "[" and whitespace are all text. Read
                ; back as written, with a flag saying it was backticked. A
                ; value or an array element only, never a name or a selector.

bareword        = bare-run *( 1*SP bare-run )
bare-run        = *bare-punct bare-unit *( *bare-punct bare-unit )
bare-punct      = ":" / ","
bare-unit       = bare-text / escape
                ; what the PARSER takes. Everything after the ":" up to an
                ; unquoted "#" is the value, trimmed at both edges of a SP,
                ; HTAB or CR only. Spaces inside are kept as typed, so
                ; "q: needs no quotes" is fine. Any other whitespace (a tab or
                ; another White_Space character), a quote, a backtick, a "["
                ; or a "]" is E025, so "k: it's" is an error. A ":" or ","
                ; needs a character other than whitespace right after it:
                ; t: 12:30, d: :0 and o: rw,noatime are text. A ":" before a
                ; blank or the end is E025 (h: a.com port: 80), and a "," there
                ; is E026, since an array is written in brackets. Only a
                ; leading "[" opens an array.

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Escapes
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

escape          = %x25C9 escape-name %x25C9
code-point      = code-prefix 1*6HEXDIG
                ; Names are a closed list and ignore case. Anything else
                ; between two marks, and a mark with no partner, is E023, and
                ; so is a code point naming a surrogate (D800-DFFF) or a value
                ; past 10FFFF. Escapes work in names, values, array elements
                ; and selector bodies, bare or quoted, but not in comments,
                ; backtick values, fence labels or raw bodies. The writer uses
                ; the first name of each and U+ with at least four digits.

fmt-bareword    = *fmt-punct fmt-unit *( *fmt-punct fmt-unit )
fmt-punct       = ":" / ","
fmt-unit        = fmt-bare-char [ variation-sel ] / %x200C-200D / subdivision-flag
                ; the minimally-quoted shape the FORMATTER emits, reached from
                ; nothing above. A value holding anything outside this class,
                ; ending in ":" or ",", or a run that would read as a fence
                ; comes back quoted. The writer quotes a value with a space,
                ; though the parser takes one bare. A ":" or "," inside stays
                ; bare (2:30PM, localhost:8080, rw,noatime). An array element
                ; with a "," is quoted, since there it splits.
subdivision-flag= %x1F3F4 3*7flag-tag %xE007F
flag-tag        = %xE0030-E0039 / %xE0061-E007A
; gen-escapes.py: begin
; Generated from Unicode 18.0 by cicd/utility/gen-escapes.py. Edit the script, not this block.
fmt-bare-char   = %x21 / %x24-26 / %x28-2B / %x2D-39 / %x3B-5A / %x5C
                / %x5E-5F / %x61-7E / %xA1-AC / %xAE-34E / %x350-61B
                / %x61D-115E / %x1161-167F / %x1681-17B3 / %x17B6-180A
                / %x1810-1FFF / %x2010-2027 / %x2030-205E / %x2070-25C8
                / %x25CA-2FFF / %x3001-3163 / %x3165-FDFF / %xFE10-FEFE
                / %xFF00-FF9F / %xFFA1-FFEF / %xFFFC-1BC9F
                / %x1BCA4-1D172 / %x1D17B-DFFFF / %xE1000-10FFFF
variation-sel   = %x180B-180D / %x180F / %xFE00-FE0F / %xE0100-E01EF
bare-text       = %x0-8 / %xE-1F / %x21 / %x24-26 / %x28-2B / %x2D-39
                / %x3B-5A / %x5C / %x5E-5F / %x61-84 / %x86-9F
                / %xA1-167F / %x1681-1FFF / %x200B-2027 / %x202A-202E
                / %x2030-205E / %x2060-25C8 / %x25CA-2FFF
                / %x3001-10FFFF
sel-text        = %x0-8 / %xE-1F / %x21 / %x24-26 / %x2A-2B / %x2D-39
                / %x3B-5A / %x5C / %x5E-5F / %x61-84 / %x86-9F
                / %xA1-167F / %x1681-1FFF / %x200B-2027 / %x202A-202E
                / %x2030-205E / %x2060-25C8 / %x25CA-2FFF
                / %x3001-10FFFF
escape-name     = "NUL" / "NULL" / "BEL" / "BELL" / "BACKSPACE" / "BS"
                / "TAB" / "HT" / "HORIZONTAL_TAB" / "NEWLINE" / "LF"
                / "LINEFEED" / "LINE_FEED" / "NEW_LINE" / "VT"
                / "VERTICAL_TAB" / "VERTICALTAB" / "FF" / "FORM_FEED"
                / "FORMFEED" / "CR" / "CARRIAGERETURN"
                / "CARRIAGE_RETURN" / "CRLF" / "CARRIAGERETURN_LINEFEED"
                / "CARRIAGE_RETURN_LINE_FEED" / "ESC" / "ESCAPE" / "DEL"
                / "DELETE" / "SPACE" / "SINGLE_QUOTE" / "SQUOTE"
                / "S_QUOTE" / "SINGLEQUOTE" / "DOUBLE_QUOTE" / "DQUOTE"
                / "D_QUOTE" / "DOUBLEQUOTE" / "BACK_TICK" / "BACKTICK"
                / "TICK" / "ESCAPE_CHAR" / "FISHEYE"
                / code-point
code-prefix     = "U+" / "UNICODE+" / "UNICODE-" / "UNICODE_"
                / "UNICODE" / "U-" / "U_" / "U"
; gen-escapes.py: end
                ; fmt-bare-char excludes whitespace, "," ":" "#" DQUOTE "'"
                ; "[" "]" a backtick and the escape mark, and each character
                ; the formatter writes as an escape (spec.md, "Escapes"). "\"
                ; is in: the formatter leaves a backslash bare. The joiners, a
                ; variation selector after a visible character and the tags of
                ; a subdivision flag are written as they are, so fmt-bareword
                ; places them. bare-text is every character but White_Space
                ; and the same reserved set.

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Stacked lists
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The other spelling of an array: an empty-valued field whose child lines are
; all "- item" is identical to that field with the bracket array of the same
; items. Context-sensitive (indentation), enforced in prose (spec.md,
; "Arrays"):
;   - opens only under a field-line whose inline value is EMPTY;
;   - uniform-or-nothing: at that child indent every line is an item or none;
;   - one item per line; a "," before a blank or the end is E026, not a split;
;   - scalars only: an item has no "field: value" children, and an item that
;     is an array is E019.
; "-" is not a name-char that can start a bare name, and the blank after it
; is required, so an item never reads as a field line. A line led by "*", the
; old marker, is E013.

list-item-line  = indent "-" 1*blank list-item *blank [ comment ]
list-item       = quoted / backticked / bareword
                ; a bare item takes spaces as a value does. A ":" before a
                ; blank or the end is E027 (- name:, - name: value), since that
                ; is how YAML starts an object in a list. - "name:" is the
                ; text, and - localhost:8080 is fine.

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Raw (fenced) blocks
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Fence termination and base-indent stripping are context-sensitive (spec.md).
; A block opens with >=3 identical fence chars; it closes at the first later
; line whose trimmed text is a run of the SAME char, length >= the opener.

fence-line      = indent fence-open *blank [ comment ]
                ; a value line for the parent field (spec.md): fills the parent's
                ; empty value, or creates a new instance of the parent if it
                ; already has one - same rule as a repeated leaf.
fence-open      = fence-run [ info-string ]
fence-run       = 3*"`" / 3*"~"                 ; run length N captured for close-match
info-string     = *blank 1*info-char *( *blank 1*info-char )
                                               ; optional label (e.g. a language), read
                                               ; as bare text: a "#" ends it and opens
                                               ; the line's comment, in either spelling,
                                               ; so ```c# labels the block c
info-char       = %x00-08 / %x0B-0C / %x0E-1F / %x21-22 / %x24-10FFFF
                ; any char but a blank, LF and "#". Wider than fmt-bare-char
                ; on purpose: ":", ",", quotes, "[" and the escape mark are
                ; plain text in a label (```sql:pg, "v" [x] reads back as
                ; written, and SetRaw writes it).
raw-content-line= *non-newline                 ; verbatim; only while a fence is open
; fence-close (context): indent (N*"`" / N*"~") *blank   where char matches opener
;                        and length >= opener length.

;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Terminals
;-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

blank           = SP / HTAB / CR               ; a carriage return is a blank outside a raw
                                               ; body: trimmed at an edge, content mid-piece,
                                               ; never indent
non-newline     = %x00-09 / %x0B-10FFFF        ; any char except LF
newline         = *CR LF                       ; LF or CRLF; a line's whole trailing CR run
                                               ; is removed
SQUOTE          = %x27
